1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,672
It's the twenty eighth.

2
00:00:01,572 --> 00:00:03,110
Rent is due in three days.

3
00:00:03,310 --> 00:00:04,507
You open your bank app.

4
00:00:04,407 --> 00:00:05,271
And you're calm.

5
00:00:05,621 --> 00:00:07,567
Then your phone buzzes. It's Jake.

6
00:00:07,767 --> 00:00:10,092
Can you spot me two hundred until Friday?

7
00:00:10,442 --> 00:00:11,750
Here's the funny part.

8
00:00:11,650 --> 00:00:14,276
Jake earns fifty five hundred dollars a month.

9
00:00:14,476 --> 00:00:16,027
You earn thirty five hundred.

10
00:00:16,377 --> 00:00:18,454
And he's the one who's broke. Again.

11
00:00:18,804 --> 00:00:21,091
Surveys keep finding that around half of

12
00:00:20,991 --> 00:00:23,551
Americans say they live paycheck to paycheck.

13
00:00:23,751 --> 00:00:26,017
Even plenty of people with good salaries.

14
00:00:26,367 --> 00:00:27,685
You used to be one of them.

15
00:00:27,885 --> 00:00:29,658
Here's exactly how you got out.

16
00:00:29,558 --> 00:00:30,738
And how you can too.

17
00:00:31,088 --> 00:00:32,600
Two years ago, you were Jake.

18
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:34,372
Payday felt like a party.

19
00:00:34,272 --> 00:00:36,316
The last week felt like survival.

20
00:00:36,666 --> 00:00:39,190
Your account hit zero around day twenty six.

21
00:00:39,390 --> 00:00:40,584
Every single month.

22
00:00:40,934 --> 00:00:43,602
Then your car needed a six hundred dollar repair.

23
00:00:43,802 --> 00:00:45,552
It went straight onto a credit card.

24
00:00:45,902 --> 00:00:47,557
That night, you finally saw it.

25
00:00:47,757 --> 00:00:49,525
The problem wasn't your income.

26
00:00:49,425 --> 00:00:51,031
It was that there was no gap

27
00:00:50,931 --> 00:00:53,128
between what came in and what went out.

28
00:00:53,478 --> 00:00:56,272
So one Sunday, you wrote down every fixed bill.

29
00:00:56,472 --> 00:00:59,997
Rent. Phone. Insurance. Utilities. Minimum payments.

30
00:01:00,347 --> 00:01:02,646
Then you went through three months of bank statements.

31
00:01:02,846 --> 00:01:05,739
Every coffee. Every delivery. Every subscription.

32
00:01:06,089 --> 00:01:08,349
The result hurt. Food delivery alone was

33
00:01:08,249 --> 00:01:10,563
about one hundred eighty dollars a month.

34
00:01:10,913 --> 00:01:13,331
Three streaming services you barely watched.

35
00:01:13,531 --> 00:01:15,837
And a gym you hadn't visited since March.

36
00:01:16,187 --> 00:01:17,713
You didn't cut all the fun.

37
00:01:17,613 --> 00:01:19,880
You kept one streaming service and cooked

38
00:01:19,780 --> 00:01:21,201
a few more nights a week.

39
00:01:21,401 --> 00:01:23,848
Suddenly, about one hundred fifty dollars a

40
00:01:23,748 --> 00:01:24,667
month was free.

41
00:01:25,017 --> 00:01:27,209
Next came the rule that changed everything.

42
00:01:27,409 --> 00:01:28,574
Pay yourself first.

43
00:01:28,924 --> 00:01:31,530
On payday, ten percent leaves automatically.

44
00:01:31,730 --> 00:01:33,283
Three hundred fifty dollars,

45
00:01:33,183 --> 00:01:35,411
straight into a separate savings account.

46
00:01:35,761 --> 00:01:37,637
Add the one hundred fifty you freed up.

47
00:01:37,837 --> 00:01:39,939
That's five hundred dollars a month you

48
00:01:39,839 --> 00:01:40,709
never even see.

49
00:01:41,059 --> 00:01:42,464
Jake does the opposite.

50
00:01:42,664 --> 00:01:44,928
He spends first, and saves whatever is

51
00:01:44,828 --> 00:01:46,238
left. Which is nothing.

52
00:01:46,588 --> 00:01:47,895
Your first goal was small.

53
00:01:48,095 --> 00:01:51,223
One thousand dollars. A starter emergency fund.

54
00:01:51,573 --> 00:01:53,069
Two months later, you hit it.

55
00:01:53,269 --> 00:01:54,388
And then, of course,

56
00:01:54,288 --> 00:01:55,661
the car broke down again.

57
00:01:56,011 --> 00:01:57,644
This time, you paid in cash.

58
00:01:57,844 --> 00:02:00,408
No credit card. No panic. Just paid.

59
00:02:00,758 --> 00:02:02,977
Jake got a surprise bill that same month.

60
00:02:03,177 --> 00:02:04,388
It went on his card,

61
00:02:04,288 --> 00:02:06,166
at over twenty percent interest.

62
00:02:06,516 --> 00:02:07,882
Then you set a bigger goal.

63
00:02:08,082 --> 00:02:09,434
Get one full month ahead.

64
00:02:09,784 --> 00:02:11,879
That means this month's bills get paid

65
00:02:11,779 --> 00:02:13,140
with last month's money.

66
00:02:13,340 --> 00:02:14,998
Payday stops being a rescue.

67
00:02:15,348 --> 00:02:16,753
At five hundred a month,

68
00:02:16,653 --> 00:02:19,309
saving another thirty five hundred dollars took

69
00:02:19,209 --> 00:02:20,342
about seven months.

70
00:02:20,692 --> 00:02:22,373
It wasn't exciting. Honestly,

71
00:02:22,273 --> 00:02:23,627
it was a little boring.

72
00:02:23,827 --> 00:02:25,953
But every month, the cushion got bigger.

73
00:02:26,303 --> 00:02:27,723
And then one day it happened.

74
00:02:27,923 --> 00:02:29,852
Payday came, and you didn't need it.

75
00:02:30,202 --> 00:02:32,226
One last trick. You opened a second

76
00:02:32,126 --> 00:02:33,986
checking account just for bills.

77
00:02:34,336 --> 00:02:36,568
Every payday, the exact amount for your

78
00:02:36,468 --> 00:02:37,552
bills moves there.

79
00:02:37,752 --> 00:02:40,470
Rent and utilities come out of it automatically.

80
00:02:40,820 --> 00:02:42,924
Whatever is left in your main account

81
00:02:42,824 --> 00:02:43,899
is yours to spend.

82
00:02:44,099 --> 00:02:46,360
No mental math. No surprises.

83
00:02:46,710 --> 00:02:48,102
Fast forward one year.

84
00:02:48,302 --> 00:02:50,762
Your company cuts overtime for three months.

85
00:02:51,112 --> 00:02:53,359
Jake panics. His card is maxed out.

86
00:02:53,559 --> 00:02:55,897
He's picking up weekend shifts just to keep up.

87
00:02:56,247 --> 00:02:57,957
You? You trim your spending a little

88
00:02:57,857 --> 00:02:58,628
and keep going.

89
00:02:58,828 --> 00:03:00,350
Your cushion covers the gap.

90
00:03:00,700 --> 00:03:02,111
Same city. Same rent.

91
00:03:02,011 --> 00:03:03,984
Jake even earns more than you.

92
00:03:04,184 --> 00:03:06,214
The only difference is the gap between

93
00:03:06,114 --> 00:03:07,891
what comes in, and what goes out.

94
00:03:08,241 --> 00:03:09,381
So here's your plan.

95
00:03:09,581 --> 00:03:11,706
This weekend, write down your fixed bills

96
00:03:11,606 --> 00:03:13,386
and look at last month's spending.

97
00:03:13,736 --> 00:03:15,344
Find three leaks you won't miss.

98
00:03:15,544 --> 00:03:16,877
And cancel them tonight.

99
00:03:17,227 --> 00:03:19,790
Then set up an automatic transfer for payday.

100
00:03:19,990 --> 00:03:21,601
Even fifty dollars counts.

101
00:03:21,951 --> 00:03:23,868
First goal, one thousand dollars.

102
00:03:24,068 --> 00:03:25,366
Then one month ahead.

103
00:03:25,266 --> 00:03:26,449
One step at a time.

104
00:03:26,799 --> 00:03:28,508
Next month, on the twenty eighth,

105
00:03:28,408 --> 00:03:29,776
you'll open your bank app.

106
00:03:29,976 --> 00:03:31,128
And you'll be calm too.

107
00:03:31,478 --> 00:03:33,465
If this helped, subscribe for a new

108
00:03:33,365 --> 00:03:34,974
simple money move every day.

109
00:03:35,174 --> 00:03:36,667
And tell me in the comments.

110
00:03:36,567 --> 00:03:38,209
What day of the month does your

111
00:03:38,109 --> 00:03:39,303
money usually run out?
